# Bitcraftly Global Accessibility (A11y) Standards

Accessibility is a mandatory engineering requirement for the Bitcraftly Platform.

These standards always apply together with:

- Bitcraftly-Engineering-Standards.mdc
- Bitcraftly-Architecture-Protection-Rules.mdc

Accessibility must be implemented during development, not added during QA.

A feature is not complete until accessibility requirements are satisfied.

---

# Target Standard

Target **WCAG 2.2 AA** compliance wherever practical.

Accessibility applies to:

- Marketing pages
- Product pages
- Dashboard
- CMS
- CRM
- AI modules
- Shared UI components
- Future features

---

# Semantic HTML

Always use semantic elements whenever possible.

Preferred elements:

- header
- nav
- main
- section
- article
- aside
- footer
- button
- form
- label
- fieldset
- legend

Do not replace semantic elements with generic `div` or `span` when native HTML provides the correct meaning.

---

# Headings

- Exactly one `h1` per page.
- Follow a logical heading hierarchy.
- Never skip heading levels.
- Headings should describe the section content.

---

# Landmarks

Prefer native landmarks:

- header
- nav
- main
- aside
- footer

Use ARIA landmark roles only when native HTML cannot express the structure.

---

# Keyboard Accessibility

Every interactive element must be usable with a keyboard.

Support:

- Tab
- Shift + Tab
- Enter
- Space
- Escape
- Arrow Keys (where applicable)

Never create keyboard traps.

---

# Focus Management

- Always provide visible focus.
- Never remove outlines unless replaced with a stronger `:focus-visible` style.
- Restore focus after closing dialogs, drawers, and menus.
- Preserve logical focus order.

---

# ARIA

Prefer semantic HTML over ARIA.

Use ARIA only when required.

Common attributes:

- aria-label
- aria-labelledby
- aria-describedby
- aria-expanded
- aria-controls
- aria-current
- aria-live
- aria-hidden

Never add unnecessary ARIA attributes.

---

# Images & Icons

Informative images:

- Meaningful alt text.

Decorative images:

- alt=""
- or aria-hidden="true"

Icon-only controls:

- Visible label
- or aria-label

---

# Buttons & Links

Buttons must:

- Have accessible names.
- Indicate loading with `aria-busy` where appropriate.
- Use native `disabled`.

Links must:

- Clearly describe the destination.
- Avoid vague labels like:
  - Click here
  - Read more
  - Learn more

---

# Forms

Every form should:

- Use labels for every input.
- Associate errors with `aria-describedby`.
- Clearly announce required fields.
- Preserve keyboard usability.

---

# Color & Contrast

- Never rely on color alone.
- Pair status with text and/or icons.
- Maintain WCAG contrast ratios.
- Preserve readability in light and dark themes.

---

# Motion

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Reduce or disable:

- Large animations
- Auto scrolling
- Parallax
- Heavy transforms

Avoid flashing or seizure-inducing effects.

---

# Common UI Patterns

## Accordion

- aria-expanded
- aria-controls
- Keyboard support

## Modal

- Focus trap
- Escape closes
- Restore focus
- aria-modal="true"

## Drawer

- Keyboard accessible
- Escape closes
- Restore focus

## Tooltip

- Available on hover
- Available on keyboard focus

## Tabs

- Arrow key navigation
- Proper ARIA relationships

## Tables

- caption
- thead
- tbody
- th
- scope

## Carousel

- Keyboard navigation
- Pause control
- Screen-reader friendly

---

# Responsive Design

Support:

- Desktop
- Laptop
- Tablet
- Mobile
- 200% browser zoom

Layouts must remain usable without horizontal scrolling where practical.

---

# React & Next.js

- Prefer semantic JSX.
- Server Components should preserve semantic structure.
- Client Components must remain keyboard accessible after hydration.
- Interactive components must preserve focus correctly after state updates.

---

# Performance

Accessibility improvements should not introduce unnecessary JavaScript or significant rendering overhead.

Prefer native browser behavior whenever possible.

---

# AI Behaviour

When generating UI:

- Prefer semantic HTML first.
- Never sacrifice accessibility for animation.
- Never remove accessibility attributes to simplify code.
- Follow existing accessibility patterns already present in the project.

---

# Verification Checklist

Before completing a task verify:

- Keyboard navigation works.
- Screen reader order is logical.
- Focus is visible.
- Heading hierarchy is correct.
- Contrast requirements are satisfied.
- Reduced motion is respected.
- Semantic HTML is used.
- Responsive layout remains accessible.
- Interactive components remain accessible after hydration.

---

# Definition of Done

Accessibility is complete only when:

- WCAG requirements are satisfied.
- Keyboard navigation works.
- Screen readers receive meaningful information.
- Focus management is correct.
- Responsive layouts remain accessible.
- Existing accessibility standards are preserved.

Required validation:

```bash
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm run build
```

Accessibility review must pass before the feature is considered complete.